WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.042
A SELECT query can be read almost like a sentence.

00:00:03.042 --> 00:00:08.637
SELECT tells PostgreSQL what columns or expressions should appear in the result.

00:00:08.637 --> 00:00:11.192
FROM tells it which table supplies the rows.

00:00:11.192 --> 00:00:14.472
WHERE can remove rows that do not meet a condition.

00:00:14.472 --> 00:00:20.157
ORDER BY controls presentation order, and LIMIT can keep only a chosen number of rows.

00:00:20.157 --> 00:00:23.187
You do not need every clause in every query.

00:00:23.187 --> 00:00:26.422
Start with the smallest version that answers the question.

00:00:26.422 --> 00:00:32.997
A good beginner habit is to list the columns you actually need.

00:00:32.997 --> 00:00:38.957
SELECT star is useful while exploring, but explicit columns make the result clearer and more predictable.

00:00:38.957 --> 00:00:41.527
Before writing a query, try saying the request in plain English.

00:00:41.527 --> 00:00:45.852
Then map each part of the sentence to the appropriate SQL clause.

00:00:45.852 --> 00:00:49.482
Think of SELECT work as progressively shaping a question.

00:00:49.482 --> 00:00:57.272
First identify the rows and columns you need, then filter, derive expressions, sort, and limit for inspection.

00:00:57.272 --> 00:01:02.722
A useful habit is to test each part on a small result before adding more complexity,

00:01:02.722 --> 00:01:05.886
especially when a filter contains NULL-sensitive logic.
